{% sec 1, "From Handshake to Done", "If I've bought something, why don't I own it yet?", "From handshake to done" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think about buying a house in England. First you agree a price and "exchange contracts": from that moment the deal is legally fixed. But you don't get the keys that day. A few weeks later comes "completion", when your money goes to the seller and the house becomes yours. In between, solicitors check everything, and both sides are exposed: what if the buyer's money doesn't arrive, or the seller can't hand over the house?</p>
    <p>Every trade has the same two moments:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>trade date</strong> (T): buyer and seller agree the price and quantity. The deal is fixed.</li>
      <li>The <strong>settlement date</strong>: the shares and the cash actually change hands. Only now do you own them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Two words cover what happens in between. {% gl "clearing" %}Clearing{% endgl %} is checking the trade details, working out who owes what, and managing the risk until it's done. {% gl "settlement" %}Settlement{% endgl %} is the final swap of securities for cash.</p>
    <p>The gap is written as "T plus" a number of business days. US shares settle {% gl "T+1" %}T+1{% endgl %}: buy on Tuesday, own them on Wednesday. Weekends and holidays don't count. That gap is a window of risk: if one side goes bust in the middle, the other may not get what it was promised. Shortening the gap shrinks the risk, which is why markets keep moving to faster cycles.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>Agreeing a trade and finishing it are two different moments. Everything in this note exists to make sure the gap between them can't hurt anyone.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "Brokers, Dealers and Custodians", "Who sits between me and the market?", "Brokers, dealers and custodians" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Between your decision and a settled position there's a chain of firms. Each one carries a different thing: some do the <em>work</em>, some take the <em>risk</em>, and some provide the <em>trust</em>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>{% gl "broker" %}Broker{% endgl %} (agent)</th><th>{% gl "dealer" %}Dealer{% endgl %} (principal)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Everyday version</td><td>An estate agent: finds you a seller, takes a fee</td><td>A car dealership: buys and sells cars from its own stock</td></tr>
        <tr><td>What it does</td><td>Sends your order to the market and works it for you</td><td>Trades <em>with</em> you, from its own book</td></tr>
        <tr><td>How it's paid</td><td>A commission you can see</td><td>The spread, built into the price</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Who holds the risk</td><td>You do; the broker owes you best execution</td><td>The dealer, once it takes your position onto its books</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The difference is the type of deal, not the firm. Big banks run both businesses side by side, with information barriers between them. That's where regulators pay attention: a firm advising you as your agent while also trading against you as a dealer has a conflict, which is what {% gl "best execution" %}best execution{% endgl %} rules and disclosure exist to police.</p>
    <h3>Who holds the shares</h3>
    <p>Shares today are entries in a computer, not certificates in a drawer. A {% gl "central securities depository" %}central securities depository{% endgl %} (CSD) keeps the official record of who owns what: DTC in the US, Euroclear UK (CREST) in the UK, and the CSCS in Nigeria. {% gl "custody" %}Custodians{% endgl %} hold investors' assets in accounts kept separate from their own money, so that if a broker or bank fails, its clients' shares aren't swallowed up with it (you saw what happens when that goes wrong in Prime Brokerage).</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "The Clearing House in the Middle", "How can I trade with strangers without worrying they'll go bust?", "The clearing house" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Imagine a school bake sale where every child pays the teacher, and the teacher pays every baker. Nobody needs to trust anybody except the teacher. A {% gl "clearing house" %}clearing house{% endgl %}, or central counterparty (CCP), is that teacher.</p>
    <p>Once a trade is cleared, the CCP replaces the original link between buyer and seller with two new ones: it becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer. This is called {% gl "novation" %}novation{% endgl %}. Instead of checking hundreds of trading partners, each firm only has to trust one: the CCP.</p>
    <h3>How the CCP protects itself</h3>
    <p>The CCP only works if it can't fail, so it builds layers of protection, used in this order if a member goes bust (the "default waterfall"):</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>The failed member's initial margin.<span class="why">A deposit every member posts against its positions before it trades.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>Variation margin, every day.<span class="why">Gains and losses are paid in cash daily (or during the day), so losses can't pile up quietly. This is {% gl "variation margin" %}variation margin{% endgl %}.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>The default fund.<span class="why">A pot every member pays into. If one member's margin isn't enough, the {% gl "default fund" %}default fund{% endgl %} covers the rest, so the risk is shared.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>The CCP's own capital.<span class="why">The clearing house's own money is the last line.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Netting: settle the difference, not every trade</h3>
    <p>Because everyone faces the same CCP, all of a firm's trades in a stock can be added up into one figure. That's {% gl "netting" %}netting{% endgl %}. A firm that buys \(10{,}000\) shares from A, sells \(8{,}000\) to B and sells \(5{,}000\) to C doesn't make three deliveries totalling \(23{,}000\) shares. It makes one: \(+10{,}000 - 8{,}000 - 5{,}000 = -3{,}000\), so it delivers \(3{,}000\) shares and settles one net cash amount.</p>
    <p>Across a whole market the saving is enormous. The US clearing house (DTCC, through its NSCC arm) handles about \(\$2.4\) trillion of trades a day, and netting cuts what actually has to move by about \(98\%\). Without it, settling every trade one by one simply wouldn't be possible.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Netting through the clearing house</h3><p>Four firms trade the same share today. Settle every trade separately, or put them all through the clearing house and settle one net amount each. Arrows show shares moving.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="presets cs-pick" id="net-day" aria-label="Which day"></div>
      <div class="presets cs-pick" id="net-mode" aria-label="How to settle"></div>
    </div>
