{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "The Settlement Timeline", "What actually happens between T and T+1?", "The settlement timeline" %}
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th><th>What can go wrong</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1. Execution (T)</td><td>Buyer and seller agree price and quantity, on an exchange or over the counter</td><td>Nothing yet: the deal is fixed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2. Matching</td><td>Both sides confirm the details agree</td><td>A "break": wrong quantity, price or settlement date. It must be fixed before settlement</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3. Novation</td><td>The clearing house becomes the other side of each trade</td><td>From here, the CCP carries the risk of either side failing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4. Netting</td><td>All of a firm's trades are added up into one obligation per stock</td><td>About \(98\%\) of the movement disappears</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5. Settlement</td><td>Shares and cash swap at the same moment: {% gl "delivery versus payment" %}delivery versus payment{% endgl %} (DVP)</td><td>If shares aren't there, the trade fails (section 5)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>DVP is the key safety rule: the shares only move if the cash moves, at the same moment. Neither side can end up having handed over its half and received nothing.</p>
    <h3>How long it takes, market by market</h3>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Market</th><th>Cycle</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>US shares, Treasuries, corporate bonds, options</td><td>T+1</td><td>Moved from T+2 in May 2024</td></tr>
        <tr><td>UK and EU shares</td><td>T+2</td><td>Moving to T+1 on 11 October 2027, together with Switzerland</td></tr>
        <tr><td>UK government bonds (gilts)</td><td>T+1</td><td>Settle through CREST</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nigerian shares (NGX)</td><td>T+2</td><td>Moved from T+3 on 28 November 2025</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FX spot</td><td>T+2</td><td>A few pairs, like USD/CAD, settle T+1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Futures</td><td>Daily</td><td>Gains and losses paid in cash every day; final settlement at expiry</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The US move to T+1 roughly halved the risk window and cut the margin clearing houses must hold. But it squeezed everything else into a day. Trade breaks must be fixed overnight, stock lenders must process recalls faster, and foreign investors hit a timing problem: their US shares settle T+1, but the FX trade to buy the dollars still settles T+2. They have to find the dollars a day early.</p>
  </div>
