{% sec 5, "When Settlement Fails", "What if the shares don't turn up?", "When settlement fails" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A {% gl "settlement fail" %}settlement fail{% endgl %} is when one side can't deliver on the settlement date. The usual reasons:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A trade break</strong> that wasn't fixed in time: the two sides' records don't match.</li>
      <li><strong>A short seller can't deliver</strong>: the borrow fell through, or the lender recalled the shares.</li>
      <li><strong>Shares stuck elsewhere</strong>: still on their way from another trade, or in the wrong account or country.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Fails cost money. In the EU, the settlement rules (CSDR) charge daily cash penalties on failing trades. Persistent fails can lead to a <strong>buy-in</strong>: the shares are bought in the market and the failing side pays any difference. In the US, Regulation SHO forces brokers to close out fails on short sales. That's why the Short Selling note said a recall at the wrong moment can force you out of a position.</p>
    <h3>Why hedge funds care</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Short selling:</strong> a failed delivery can force a buy-in.</li>
      <li><strong>Dividends and votes:</strong> who gets them depends on who owns the shares on the record date, which depends on settlement (next note).</li>
      <li><strong>Cash:</strong> with T+1, sale proceeds arrive the next day, so the fund can't spend them today.</li>
      <li><strong>Foreign markets:</strong> different cycles in different countries create FX timing gaps.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Keeping all this running is the operations team's job: trade booking, settlement, fails, cash and margin, every day. Investors check it carefully before they invest (operational due diligence), and plenty of funds have failed it.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 6, "Cash Today, Not Profit Later", "How can a clearing house make a firm fail?", "Liquidity, not solvency" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Clearing houses make the system much safer, but they don't make risk disappear. They change its shape. Without a CCP, the danger is that the other side doesn't pay you: <strong>credit risk</strong>. With a CCP, the danger becomes having to pay cash, today, the moment prices move against you: <strong>liquidity risk</strong>.</p>
    <p>If a cleared position loses \(40{,}000\) in value today, you post \(40{,}000\) of cash as variation margin today, even if you're sure it will bounce back tomorrow. A firm can be <em>solvent</em> (worth more than it owes) and still fail because it can't find the cash in time. Think of a shop with lots of stock and loyal customers that still goes bust because it can't pay this week's rent.</p>
    <p>A famous example: on 28 January 2021, at the height of the GameStop squeeze, the US clearing house asked the trading app Robinhood for about \(\$3\) billion of extra deposit, because its customers' trades had become so large and so risky. Robinhood didn't have that much cash ready, so it stopped its customers from buying GameStop while it raised money. Its customers were furious, but the trigger was a clearing-house margin call, not a choice about the share.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 7, "And in FX, Gold and Bonds?", "How do my three markets settle?", "FX, gold and bonds" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>FX: the risk has a name</h3>
    <p>An FX trade is two payments: you send one currency and receive another. If the two payments don't happen at the same moment, one side can pay and then not get paid. This is {% gl "settlement risk" %}settlement risk{% endgl %}, and the market calls it Herstatt risk after what happened on 26 June 1974. German regulators shut a small bank, Bankhaus Herstatt, at the end of the German business day. Banks had already paid it Deutsche marks in Frankfurt, but the dollars it owed them in New York were never paid.</p>
    <p>The fix took decades. Since 2002, most big FX trades settle through {% gl "CLS" %}CLS{% endgl %}, a specialist bank that releases both currencies only when both have arrived: payment versus payment, the FX version of DVP. FX spot settles T+2. Some currencies, like the naira for foreign investors, often trade as {% gl "non-deliverable forward" %}non-deliverable forwards{% endgl %} (NDFs): no naira ever changes hands, and the difference is simply paid in dollars at the end.</p>
    <h3>Gold: settled in London's vaults</h3>
    <p>Most gold trading is "loco London unallocated": promises to deliver gold in London, settled as entries between the big bullion banks through London Precious Metals Clearing Limited. Only a small share ever becomes physical bars moving between vaults. COMEX gold futures clear through CME's clearing house, with variation margin paid every day like any future.</p>
    <h3>Bonds: CSDs and ICSDs</h3>
    <p>Gilts settle T+1 through CREST. Eurobonds (bonds sold to international investors, often in dollars, including Nigeria's) settle through the two international CSDs, Euroclear in Brussels and Clearstream in Luxembourg. US Treasuries settle T+1 through the Federal Reserve's own system.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 8, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>T+1, T+2</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>How many business days after the trade it settles.</p><p class="eg">"It's T+1 now, so the cash is in tomorrow."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Cleared / bilateral</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Through a clearing house, or directly between the two firms.</p><p class="eg">"Is this cleared or bilateral?"</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Break</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A trade where the two sides' records don't match.</p><p class="eg">"We've got a break on the quantity, ops is on it."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Fail</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A trade that didn't settle on time.</p><p class="eg">"Our short failed, we're getting bought in."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>VM / IM</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Variation margin (daily gains and losses in cash) and initial margin (the up-front deposit).</p><p class="eg">"VM call's big today, check the cash."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>DVP</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Delivery versus payment: shares and cash swap at the same moment.</p><p class="eg">"It settles DVP, so there's no free delivery."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Agency / principal</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Trading for a client as a broker, or against them as a dealer.</p><p class="eg">"Do you want it agency or on risk?"</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Value date</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>In FX, the day the currencies actually change hands.</p><p class="eg">"Spot value's Wednesday."</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where settlement turns up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: know when your cash is real</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>When you sell shares, the money isn't really yours to withdraw until settlement. Brokers often let you trade with it straight away, which hides the gap until something goes wrong. CFDs and spot FX on a trading platform never settle at all: they're just rolled forward each night, which is why there's a swap charge instead. For your own FX and gold trading, the value date and the overnight rollover are where settlement shows up.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: the slowest settlement there is</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Buying property is clearing and settlement in slow motion. Exchange of contracts is the trade date, completion is settlement, the solicitors' client accounts act as custodians, the deposit (often \(10\%\)) works like margin, and the Land Registry is the central record of who owns what. Weeks of gap mean more can go wrong: chains collapse, buyers lose their mortgage. In Nigeria, the title record is often the weak link, which is why land titles get their own note in Real Estate.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: T+2, and faster money elsewhere</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The NGX moved from T+3 to T+2 on 28 November 2025, with the CSCS as its central depository, and there's talk of going further. Meanwhile, Nigerian bank transfers through NIBSS are already instant, so cash moves faster than shares do. That gap is where fintech ideas live: share settlement in minutes on shared ledgers ("tokenisation") is one being tested around the world. Any product that lets Nigerians trade foreign shares also has to manage the FX timing gaps from section 4.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 10, "The Maths Underneath", "How do you put numbers on clearing and settlement?" %}
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Net position through a CCP (buys positive, sells negative)</span>\[ \text{net} = \sum_i q_i \qquad \text{net} &gt; 0 \Rightarrow \text{receive}, \quad \text{net} &lt; 0 \Rightarrow \text{deliver} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">How much netting saves</span>\[ \text{saving} = 1 - \frac{\sum_{\text{firms}} |\text{net}|}{\sum_{\text{trades}} |q|} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Variation margin today</span>\[ \text{VM} = \text{change in the position's value today} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Settlement date</span>\[ \text{settlement date} = T + n \text{ business days} \]</div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example</span>
    <h3>A firm buys \(10{,}000\) shares from A, sells \(8{,}000\) to B and sells \(5{,}000\) to C, all cleared.</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Trade by trade: three deliveries, \(10{,}000 + 8{,}000 + 5{,}000 = 23{,}000\) shares moving.</li>
      <li>Net: \(+10{,}000 - 8{,}000 - 5{,}000 = -3{,}000\).</li>
      <li>Saving for this firm: \(1 - \frac{3{,}000}{23{,}000} \approx 87\%\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">One delivery of \(3{,}000\) shares, against one net cash amount.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 11, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>A firm buys \(12{,}000\) shares from one counterparty and sells \(9{,}000\) to another in the same cleared stock. What is its net settlement obligation in shares (positive = receive)?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(+3{,}000\): it receives \(3{,}000\) shares.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>A cleared position loses \(40{,}000\) in value today. Variation margin settles daily in cash. How much cash must be posted today?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(40{,}000\), in cash, today, whatever you expect tomorrow.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>You sell UK shares on a Thursday. They settle T+2. When does the cash arrive?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>The following Monday: Thursday plus two business days skips Saturday and Sunday.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>A UK investor buys US shares on a Monday and pays for them by buying dollars with pounds. When are the shares due, and when do the dollars arrive?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>The shares settle Tuesday (T+1); the FX settles Wednesday (T+2). The investor must find the dollars a day early, by pre-funding or borrowing.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>What is the difference between a broker and a dealer, for the client?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>A broker acts as your agent: you pay a visible commission and keep the market risk. A dealer trades with you as principal: the cost is hidden in the spread, and the risk passes to the dealer straight away.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">6</span><span>Interview question: what is Herstatt risk, and how is it handled today?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>The risk that one side of an FX trade pays its currency but never receives the other, because the payments happen at different times in different countries. Named after Bankhaus Herstatt, closed in 1974 after receiving Deutsche marks but before paying dollars. Today CLS settles most big FX trades payment versus payment, releasing both currencies only when both have arrived.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}
